  the launch compiles extracts device-free, (c) one rank loads its compiled graph on
  one device, (d) the per-process host footprint read from that load times the world
  size fits the host memory pool the launch reserves, (e) one request passes the
  runner's request path in CPU mode - plus a forbidden effect and one pointer each in
  the purpose and the CPU-first principle. prepare_host's lease paragraph states the
  enforcement point: the hardware queue tool grants a serving-class job lease only
  when the request names the job's class, its tip and the client leg that will drive
  its requests, and the attempt log carries the tip's rung records; the job-lease
  record carries the three, so the tool reads what it refuses on - the refusal pattern
  revision 14 used for the second campaign lease; cmd_grant gains the three arguments
  in the release layer. Evidence that it changed outcomes: 74 attempt_failed rows
  against 18 candidate_serving; the first served candidate that answered a request
  came on day 21 (2026-09-16), the first charged launch on day 14; in
  artifacts/cross-run/failure-fingerprints.yaml, r8, r9 and r16 are graph-extraction
  refusals a device-free compile reproduces, r7 and r13 one-rank load refusals, r11
  and r17 host-memory collapses the footprint arithmetic predicts (release 553: 33 GB
  times 64 ranks on a 2 TB host), and attempts 17 and 18 died at the first request
  (LEAD-LOG 1628 to 1633) - eight of ten charged pre-serve failures catchable
  uncharged; the run adopted rungs (b) to (d) by hand at attempts 12 to 16 (LEAD-LOG
  1611 to 1621) and served on the next charged launch; attempt 22 held a server 4 h 15
  m charged with no client (usage record item 6). Why general: every campaign on this
  workflow compiles graphs, loads ranks, holds host memory and serves requests, and
  each failure class has a probe that costs minutes where the launch costs hours; the
  graph names the rung classes, the flags stay in the reference layer. Why not a check
  on an edge: the launch happens inside the node, so the declared enforcement point is
  the grant, which the tool already refuses on other grounds. Cost per run: five
  uncharged records per tip, no seat, in place of one charged launch per catchable
  defect. User-only change touched: hardware_attempt_counts' class rule (approved
  budgets) - diagnostic now reads "compiles no new graph on the device, serves no
  request and decides nothing about acceptance - reading state out of a served tip's
  graphs, or loading one rank of a tip's compiled graph as a bring-up rung", so rung
  (c) is uncharged as the field practised (the rb-load rows carry class diagnostic in
  the fingerprint file); the serving definition and the count of ten are untouched.

  (2) A DECLARED EXIT FROM THE LOOP FOR A PRODUCT DEFECT. New outcome
